<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 9: </span>Experiment #2 detailed results for most recent time step. Note that <span id="A3.T9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Format Selectivity</span> is an additional metric we report on here, defined as: when the model is instructed to ignore the formatting, what percentage of the time does the model correctly ignore the document’s formatting. Values below 1 are reported as <math id="A3.T9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.7 </span>Longer episode lengths</h3>

<div id="A3.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We run the same evaluation but with sequence lengths of 20 instead of 10. Performance is reported in Figure <a href="#A3.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ C.7 Longer episode lengths ‣ Appendix C ‘Continual Learning of Knowledge, Styles, and Behaviors’ Experiment Details ‣ Tell Me What To Learn: Generalizing Neural Memory to be Controllable in Natural Language" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F13" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.23201v1/figures/mixed_docs_curves_seq20.png" id="A3.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/116;" width="476" height="116" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13: </span>Performance on experiment 2 when rolling out to sequence length of 20 instead of 10.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>‘Compositional Generalization’ Experiment Details</h2>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Learning Instructions</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We focused the experiment on the 12 categories seen during training. Because we have 12 categories, and we are targeting one for fact learning and one for refusal learning, that makes up 144 possible combinations of compositional types of learning instructions. To avoid having to author 144 learning instructions, we randomly selected a third of these combinations (making up 48 possible combinations) and used GPT-5.1 to author a compositional learning instruction for the combination. All learning instructions used can be seen in Table <a href="#A4.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ D.1 Learning Instructions ‣ Appendix D ‘Compositional Generalization’ Experiment Details ‣ Tell Me What To Learn: Generalizing Neural Memory to be Controllable in Natural Language" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T10" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A4.T10.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
